WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
DALE MARKOWITZ: All right,
we are 15 minutes in.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:10.655
And-- oh, it doesn't look that
compelling, I'll be honest.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:17.810
It's the holidays.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.310
And there is nothing
that that makes

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.018
me want to do more
than machine learning.

00:00:21.018 --> 00:00:23.268
Today I'm going to build a
machine learning model that

00:00:23.268 --> 00:00:26.210
will analyze recipes and then
dream up an entirely new recipe

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.535
that I'll bake and eat
right before you here today.

00:00:28.535 --> 00:00:30.410
And to make the project
a little bit sweeter,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.675
I'll add just a sprinkle
of model explainability

00:00:32.675 --> 00:00:34.550
so that we understand
why our model is making

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.110
the predictions that it is.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.820
I'll be joined by Sara Robinson.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.483
Sara is an AI expert and
baking extraordinaire

00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:41.650
who works with me at Google.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.120
Hey, Sara.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:42.950
SARA ROBINSON: Hey, Dale.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:43.890
Thanks for having me.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:44.780
DALE MARKOWITZ: I heard
that a couple of months

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.820
ago you use a machine
learning model

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.830
to come up with a new
recipe that you baked.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:49.770
Can you tell me about that?

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.187
SARA ROBINSON: So
I've been baking

00:00:51.187 --> 00:00:52.718
a ton over the past few months.

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:54.260
And after following
a lot of recipes,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.570
I started to notice that
a lot of baking recipes

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:57.320
follow a pattern.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.237
So I started to wonder,
since machine learning

00:00:59.237 --> 00:01:00.927
is all about finding
patterns in data,

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.510
could I build a
machine learning model

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.430
to predict what
I'm baking, given

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.850
a list of ingredient amounts?

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.900
So I built a model to
predict whether a recipe was

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.612
a bread, a cake, or a cookie.

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:13.070
DALE MARKOWITZ: So we're going
to do exactly the same thing

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:13.790
here today.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.250
We'll build a model that
takes a list of ingredients

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.998
and then predicts
bread, cake, or cookie.

00:01:17.998 --> 00:01:19.790
And then we're going
to use that core model

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.192
to come up with new recipes
that we'll bake, right?

00:01:22.192 --> 00:01:23.150
SARA ROBINSON: Awesome.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:24.480
Let's get started.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.920
So step one is to collect a data
set of cookies, cake, and bread

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:28.640
recipes.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.920
We gathered about 700 recipes.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.260
Next we had to do a bit
of data pre-processing.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.330
For example, there's
a lot of consensus

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.330
that banana bread and pumpkin
bread are actually cakes.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.220
Even Paul Hollywood agrees.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.200
So we had to move the
banana and pumpkin

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.778
breads from the bread category
into the cake category.

00:01:44.778 --> 00:01:46.820
DALE MARKOWITZ: Next, we
slimmed down the recipes

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.010
to just their core ingredients,
like flour, eggs, butter,

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.120
sugar, and a couple more.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.703
SARA ROBINSON: We also converted
all of our ingredient amounts

00:01:53.703 --> 00:01:56.030
from cups and sticks and
teaspoons and tablespoons

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.800
into one single
measurement unit.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.160
We used ounces.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.050
OK, so here are
all of my recipes.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.170
They're converted into
ounces, and cleaned up

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.170
and labeled in a Google Sheet.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.920
To build my model, I'm
going to use a tool

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.870
in Google Cloud called
AutoML Tables, which

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.120
is a no-code tool for building
machine learning models

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.780
to analyze tabular data, like
spreadsheets or databases.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.910
OK, I'm opening up
Tables, and I'll

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.370
import my data from that
CSV I just showed you.

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:23.140
I have to select
the bucket where

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.348
I want the data to be stored.

00:02:24.348 --> 00:02:27.490
And it'll take a
second to import.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.553
When it's done, I can
click on my new data set.

00:02:29.553 --> 00:02:31.720
And as you can see, a couple
of stats about the data

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.240
are automatically generated,
like whether certain columns

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.420
are missing values, and also
this useful field, Correlation

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.730
With Target, which
says in isolation

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.200
how good is each
of these columns

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.550
at predicting whether or not
something is cake, bread,

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.680
or cookie.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.460
I've also selected
a target field,

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.043
which is what we're
trying to predict,

00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:50.990
which is, in this case, Type,
that I've already selected.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.360
To train a model, I'll click
the Train Model button,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.170
name my model, say how long
I want it to train for,

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.630
specify which columns
I want to include.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:02.360
I don't want to use the name
of the recipe or URLs or IDs

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.990
or anything like that.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.350
And then I click
Train Model and wait.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.060
This part could take
a couple of hours.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.440
When my model is
done training, I

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.380
can see how well it did
in the Evaluate Model tab.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.540
Here I can see lots of
stats on model accuracy.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.490
And if I scroll down to
the Confusion Matrix,

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.470
I can also see how
often my model correctly

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:20.030
predicted recipes.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.190
So it was most
accurate for bread,

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.770
which it labeled
correctly 93% of the time,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.200
and a little less accurate
for cookies, which it confused

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.928
for cake 21% of the time.

00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:31.220
When I'm ready to use my
model, I can hop into the Test

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.030
and Use tab.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.220
And here I can make predictions
right from within the UI.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.020
So I can fill in the values for
a cake recipe, click Predict,

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:39.980
and boom.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.170
The model thinks this is
cake, which is correct.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.640
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
Sara was able to build

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.190
a model that takes the recipe
and then predicts cookie, cake,

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:46.910
or bread.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.410
But the inquisitive
baker might want

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.960
to know, what is it that
makes a cookie a cookie?

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.940
What gives it that
chewy-crunchy property,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.980
that cookieness, if you will?

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.875
And what makes bread
fluffy and cake spongy?

00:03:56.875 --> 00:03:59.000
In other words, we want to
model that not just made

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.500
a prediction, but
also explains what

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.910
ingredients it was looking at
when it made that prediction.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.490
And that's called
model explainability.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:05.365
SARA ROBINSON: Right.

00:04:05.365 --> 00:04:07.753
We want to understand how
important each ingredient is

00:04:07.753 --> 00:04:09.920
to the model when it's
deciding whether something is

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.958
a bread, a cookie, or a cake.

00:04:12.958 --> 00:04:15.250
I can take a look at Feature
Importance, which tells me

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.875
how important different
ingredients are

00:04:16.875 --> 00:04:18.920
to the overall
model's predictions.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.130
So in this case,
butter, sugar, and yeast

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.863
were really good at helping
differentiate the baked goods.

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:26.530
Now, even though butter might be
important to the overall model,

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.613
it might be that different
ingredients matter more

00:04:28.613 --> 00:04:29.950
depending on the recipe.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.990
So if I go into the
Test and Use tab,

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.010
I'm going to put
in a cake recipe.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:37.150
And then I'll hit this Generate
Feature Importance box,

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:38.137
hit Predict.

00:04:38.137 --> 00:04:39.970
And then I'll be able
to see how ingredients

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.310
matter for a specific recipe.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.768
Here egg and fat got the highest
feature importance scores,

00:04:44.768 --> 00:04:47.018
so we can assume that they're
the most important thing

00:04:47.018 --> 00:04:49.560
the model is looking at when it
decided that this was a cake.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.060
DALE MARKOWITZ: OK,
so now I'm going

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.400
to try something really radical.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.820
I'm going to bake not a
cookie or cake, but something

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.130
that our model deems to be
a split between the two,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.132
a "cakie," if you will.

00:04:58.132 --> 00:05:00.340
SARA ROBINSON: And I'm going
to bake something that's

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.200
a half bread, half cookie.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.910
I'm going to call
it a "breakie."

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.730
To find a breakie recipe,
I adjusted the ingredients

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.980
in the model UI until
our model predicted

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.190
that our recipe was about
equal parts cookie and bread.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.335
Same for Dale's cakie.

00:05:13.335 --> 00:05:15.460
DALE MARKOWITZ: All right,
Sara, you ready to bake?

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.300
SARA ROBINSON: Yes.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:16.990
Let's do it.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.678
On your marks, get set, bake.

00:05:19.678 --> 00:05:22.595
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:22.595 --> 00:05:24.970
DALE MARKOWITZ: None of my
training prepared me for this.

00:05:30.775 --> 00:05:31.275
Um.

00:05:37.903 --> 00:05:39.070
All right, here's the cakie.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.440
Good luck, little cake--

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:49.940
cookie.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:51.725
Cakie.

00:05:51.725 --> 00:05:54.920
SARA ROBINSON: Here
is what we've got.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:56.000
DALE MARKOWITZ: OK.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.038
I guess I'll try to
pry it from the pan.

00:05:59.038 --> 00:06:00.830
SARA ROBINSON: It does
taste like a cookie,

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.570
but it is a little
bit more airy.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.240
DALE MARKOWITZ: I can't tell
if it's cooked all the way.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.680
SARA ROBINSON: [LAUGHING]

00:06:09.632 --> 00:06:11.584
[THUMPING]

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.395
DALE MARKOWITZ: Did not
work exactly the way

00:06:18.395 --> 00:06:20.230
I had hoped it might work.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:22.738
SARA ROBINSON: This is
actually pretty good.

00:06:22.738 --> 00:06:26.610
[TRUMPET FANFARE]

00:06:30.395 --> 00:06:32.020
DALE MARKOWITZ: OK,
it's kind of nasty.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.430
SARA ROBINSON: So, yeah,
I guess it's a success.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:38.150
DALE MARKOWITZ: I wonder if I
put the right amount of flour

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:41.400
or double the amount of flour.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.550
So I feel I kind of flubbed
that recipe, something

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:44.175
with the flour.

00:06:44.175 --> 00:06:46.050
But Sara then went back
and baked it herself,

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.520
and her cakie came
out just fine.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.812
So I guess we'll have to
attribute that to human error.

00:06:51.602 --> 00:06:53.560
If you want to know the
details of this project

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.510
and steal our recipes,
make sure you check out

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.550
the blog post linked below.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.420
Happy holidays, and
see you next year.

